    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm working on a schema migration and i'm a bit stuck on the normalization part. can you walk me through how to actually get a table from 2NF to 3NF? i get the basic idea of removing redundancy but i keep tripping up on how to identify the dependencies. thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! To transition from 2NF to 3NF, you first need to ensure the table is already in 2NF by having all fields partially dependent on a candidate key. If there's transitive dependency, you'll need to eliminate it. For example, if you have a table with attributes Course (CourseID, CourseName) and Instructor (CourseID, InstructorName, OfficeLocation) where CourseID is the primary key, InstructorName and OfficeLocation are dependent on CourseID but not directly related, which is a transitive dependency. You can refactor the design by splitting the table into separate Instructor table linked by the CourseID.\n\n1. Identify the tables involved - Instructor and Course Tables.\n2. Examine the relationships and the attributes in each table.\n3. Split the tables to eliminate the transitive dependency, for example, creating a prerequisite attribute for each Course in the Course Table and Instructor in the Instructor Table.\n4. Update the relationships - now you have a one-to-many relationship from Course to Instructor, which means that a single Course can have multiple Instructors without repeating CourseIDs.\n\nKeep in mind that the reduction in redundancy and elimination of the transitive dependency are key to achieving 3NF. What specifics are confusing you about",
      "source": "target",
