      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. i think i get the transitive thing now. can you give me a slightly more complex example though? like something involving a few more tables or different types of keys? i want to make sure i can spot them when the dependencies aren't just straight lines.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely! Let's consider a more complex scenario with three tables in a university database: Student, Course, and Contact. Each table has its primary key: Student(StudentID, StudentName), Course(CourseID, CourseName, CourseDescription), and Contact(ContactID, Mobile, Email), where ContactID also happens to reference the Student (beyond the primary key).\n\nThe transitive dependency here involves Contact.\n\n1. Assess the relationships:\n   - Student to Contact (one-to-one)\n   - Student to Course (many-to-many, hence requires a junction table, e.g., Enrollment)\n   - Course to Contact (none)\n\n2. To move to 3NF, we can adjust the Contact table:\n   - Keep StudentID as the foreign key to the Student table.\n   - Remove the StudentName attribute from Contact, as it's dependent on StudentID (transitively).\n   - Add a new primary key to Contact, perhaps Email or Mobile (if no duplicates exist), or just retain ContactID.\n\n3. Update your relationships accordingly:\n   - Create a junction table between Student and Course, if not done already.\n\n4. Verify the normalization success:\n   - Student: now has two attributes only, StudentID and StudentName.",
